From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 05:58:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 04/37] LLHD Sig2Reg: a bare probe of the driven signal writes
 nothing

tryNarrowFeeder handled array_inject chains, concats and mux trees, but not a
feeder that is exactly llhd.prb of the signal being driven. That is a pure
hold -- it writes nothing -- and treating it as unnarrowable poisoned the
whole mux union, forcing the drive back to full width and making two drives
of DISJOINT bits look identical.

OpenC910's ct_fifo writes one bit of a 2-bit valid vector per entry and spells
'no write this cycle' exactly that way:

    %38 = comb.mux %34, %35, %37
      %35 = comb.concat %32, %true        // bit 0 written
      %37 = comb.mux %36, %33, %70
         %70 = llhd.prb %fifo_entry_vld   // writes nothing
      %32 = comb.extract %70 from 1       // pass-through of bit 1

Restricted to a probe of THIS signal; a probe of any other signal is a real
value being written and keeps its full-width interval. An all-empty union is
also refused rather than emitting zero intervals, since dropping a drive
outright would silently remove logic -- a failure mode this project has had to
retract twice.

OpenC910: refused signals 40 -> 15 (13 overlapping + 2 genuine latches),
residual llhd ops 271 -> 85, modules holding LLHD 35 -> 10 of 503, dropped
logic 6.0% -> 3.2% of IR ops.

Also corrects a retracted reading: the earlier claim that the remaining
failures showed 'two granularities that genuinely overlap' came from parsing
the debug log without splitting per-signal blocks, so intervals from
mst_penable_flop/mst_pwrite_flop were attributed to mst_pprot_flop. Parsed
per-signal, 37 of 38 had every interval at the same offset and width.

ibex unaffected: core.mlir, core_deagg.mlir, struct.mlir byte-identical.
---
 lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+)

diff --git a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp
index 5b4624804..2716f8e84 100644
--- a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp
+++ b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp
@@ -253,6 +253,24 @@ tryNarrowFeeder(Value feeder, llhd::SignalOp sigOp, Value selfFeedback,
                 unsigned depth = 0) {
   feeder = unwrapClockEnable(feeder, selfFeedback);
 
+  // A feeder that is exactly a probe of the signal being driven is a pure
+  // hold: it writes nothing. Without this the arm poisons the whole mux
+  // union below and the drive falls back to full width, which is what made
+  // two drives of DISJOINT bits look identical. OpenC910's `ct_fifo` writes
+  // one bit of a 2-bit valid vector per entry and expresses "no write this
+  // cycle" exactly this way:
+  //
+  //     %38 = comb.mux %34, %35, %37
+  //       %35 = comb.concat %32, %true        // bit 0 written
+  //       %37 = comb.mux %36, %33, %70
+  //          %70 = llhd.prb %fifo_entry_vld   // <- writes nothing
+  //
+  // Restricted to a probe of THIS signal; a probe of any other signal is a
+  // real value being written and must keep its full-width interval.
+  if (auto probeOp = feeder.getDefiningOp<llhd::ProbeOp>())
+    if (probeOp.getSignal() == sigOp.getResult())
+      return SmallVector<NarrowedWrite>{};
+
   if (auto narrowed = tryNarrowArrayInjectChain(feeder, sigOp))
     return narrowed;
   if (auto narrowed = tryNarrowBitConcatChain(feeder, sigOp))
@@ -277,6 +295,13 @@ tryNarrowFeeder(Value feeder, llhd::SignalOp sigOp, Value selfFeedback,
   if (!rhs)
     return std::nullopt;
 
+  // An empty union means the drive provably writes nothing at all. Rather
+  // than emit zero intervals -- which would drop the drive and silently
+  // remove logic, a failure mode this project has had to retract twice --
+  // fall back to the conservative full-width answer.
+  if (lhs->empty() && rhs->empty())
+    return std::nullopt;
+
   SmallVector<NarrowedWrite> merged(*lhs);
   for (const auto &w : *rhs) {
     bool identical = false;
